# Approximation of the Maximum Function by a Differentiable Function

**Claim.** Yes. The function $f(x,y)=\max\{x,y\}$ on $(0,\infty)^2$ can be uniformly approximated by a differentiable (in fact $C^\infty$) function.

## Construction (the "soft-max" / log-sum-exp family)

For every $\varepsilon>0$ define
$$
g_\varepsilon(x,y)\;=\;\frac{1}{\varepsilon}\log\!\big(e^{\varepsilon x}+e^{\varepsilon y}\big),\qquad x,y>0.
$$

## 1. Smoothness

$g_\varepsilon$ is a composition of the maps $x\mapsto e^{\varepsilon x}$, $y\mapsto e^{\varepsilon y}$ (smooth), addition, the logarithm (smooth on $(0,\infty)$, and $e^{\varepsilon x}+e^{\varepsilon y}>0$ always), and multiplication by $1/\varepsilon$. Hence $g_\varepsilon\in C^\infty\big((0,\infty)^2\big)$.

## 2. Uniform approximation of $\max\{x,y\}$

Let $M=\max\{x,y\}$ and $m=\min\{x,y\}$, so $m\le M$.

**Lower bound.** Since $e^{\varepsilon x}+e^{\varepsilon y}\ge e^{\varepsilon M}$,
$$
g_\varepsilon(x,y)=\frac{1}{\varepsilon}\log\!\big(e^{\varepsilon x}+e^{\varepsilon y}\big)\ge\frac{1}{\varepsilon}\log\!\big(e^{\varepsilon M}\big)=M.
$$

**Upper bound.** Since $e^{\varepsilon x}+e^{\varepsilon y}\le 2\,e^{\varepsilon M}$,
$$
g_\varepsilon(x,y)\le\frac{1}{\varepsilon}\log\!\big(2\,e^{\varepsilon M}\big)=M+\frac{\log 2}{\varepsilon}.
$$

Combining,
$$
0\;\le\;g_\varepsilon(x,y)-\max\{x,y\}\;\le\;\frac{\log 2}{\varepsilon}\qquad\text{for all }x,y>0.
$$

The right-hand side is independent of $(x,y)$, so $g_\varepsilon\to\max\{x,y\}$ **uniformly** on $(0,\infty)^2$ as $\varepsilon\to\infty$.

## 3. Conclusion

For any prescribed tolerance $\delta>0$, choose $\varepsilon>\dfrac{\log 2}{\delta}$. Then $g_\varepsilon$ is infinitely differentiable on $(0,\infty)^2$ and satisfies
$$
\big|g_\varepsilon(x,y)-\max\{x,y\}\big|<\delta\quad\text{for every }x,y>0.
$$

Therefore the maximum function $f(x,y)=\max\{x,y\}$ on $(0,\infty)^2$ can be uniformly approximated by a differentiable function. $\blacksquare$

